scenario_questions.scenario:
You inherited a method that computes the average of integers from a text file, one value per line. It intermittently throws NumberFormatException due to blank lines and sometimes keeps the file locked on Windows, preventing deletion after the run.
```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public double avg(Path p) throws Exception {
  Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(p);
  double result = lines
      .map(Integer::parseInt) // crashes on blanks
      .mapToInt(i -> i)
      .average()
      .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("no data"));
  // later code tries to delete the file here and fails on Windows
  return result;
}
```
The file may contain blanks, extra spaces, or comment lines starting with '#'. Deletions after computing the average must succeed on all platforms.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Key points:
- Use try-with-resources so the stream (and underlying file channel) is closed deterministically before any deletion attempt.
- Preprocess lines by trimming, skipping blanks and comment lines; handle NumberFormatException locally by filtering.
- Decide the empty-case contract; e.g., throw IllegalStateException with a clear message.
Example:
```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public double avg(Path p) throws Exception {
  try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(p)) {
    return lines
        .map(String::trim)
        .filter(s -> !s.isEmpty())
        .filter(s -> !s.startsWith("#"))
        .mapToInt(s -> {
          try { return Integer.parseInt(s); }
          catch (NumberFormatException e) { return Integer.MIN_VALUE; }
        })
        .filter(v -> v != Integer.MIN_VALUE)
        .average()
        .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("no valid numbers"));
  }
}
```
Rationale: try-with-resources closes the stream before returning; parsing is robust to blanks, spaces, and comments; invalid lines are skipped; the empty-case behavior is explicit.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A deduplication job reads millions of Order objects and inserts them into a HashSet to remove duplicates before further processing. The job's memory usage grows unexpectedly and the output still contains duplicates with the same id and timestamp.
```java
import java.util.*;
class Order {
  final String id; final long ts; final String payload;
  Order(String id, long ts, String payload) { this.id=id; this.ts=ts; this.payload=payload; }
  @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (!(o instanceof Order)) return false;
    Order other = (Order)o;
    return Objects.equals(id, other.id) && ts == other.ts; // duplicates defined by (id, ts)
  }
  // hashCode not overridden
}

Set<Order> unique = new HashSet<>();
// stream of orders ... each added to 'unique'
```
The team also needs iteration order to match insertion order for downstream reconciliation logs.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Bug: equals is overridden but hashCode is not, violating the general contract used by hash-based collections; equal objects may occupy different buckets, causing duplicates and poor performance.
Fix: Implement a consistent hashCode using the same fields as equals; choose LinkedHashSet to preserve insertion order.
Example:
```java
class Order {
  final String id; final long ts; final String payload;
  Order(String id, long ts, String payload) { this.id=id; this.ts=ts; this.payload=payload; }
  @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Order)) return false;
    Order other = (Order)o;
    return ts == other.ts && Objects.equals(id, other.id);
  }
  @Override public int hashCode() {
    return Objects.hash(id, ts);
  }
}

Set<Order> unique = new LinkedHashSet<>();
```
Justification: equals/hashCode consistency ensures logical duplicates collide and one is retained; LinkedHashSet preserves insertion order with minimal extra overhead versus HashSet.

A compact immutable value object with final fields, defensive checks in constructor, and value-based equals/hashCode. Example:
```java
import java.util.*;

public final class Range {
  private final int start;
  private final int end;
  public Range(int start, int end) {
    if (start > end) throw new IllegalArgumentException("start > end");
    this.start = start; this.end = end;
  }
  public int start() { return start; }
  public int end() { return end; }
  public boolean contains(int x) { return x >= start && x <= end; }
  public Optional<Range> mergeIfOverlapping(Range other) {
    if (other == null) throw new NullPointerException("other");
    if (other.end < this.start - 0 || other.start > this.end + 0) {
      // not overlapping or adjacent
      if (other.end < this.start - 1 || other.start > this.end + 1) return Optional.empty();
    }
    // Overlap or adjacency: [minStart, maxEnd]
    int ns = Math.min(this.start, other.start);
    int ne = Math.max(this.end, other.end);
    if (other.end < this.start - 1 || other.start > this.end + 1) return Optional.empty();
    return Optional.of(new Range(ns, ne));
  }
  @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Range)) return false;
    Range r = (Range)o;
    return start == r.start && end == r.end;
  }
  @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(start, end); }
  @Override public String toString() { return "["+start+","+end+"]"; }
}
```
Notes: final class + final fields ensures immutability; constructor enforces invariant; equals/hashCode support proper behavior in sets/maps; merge considers adjacency (e.g., [1,3] and [4,5]) as mergeable.

Stream the file line-by-line, normalize, filter comments, split into tokens, and update a mutable frequency map, or use groupingBy with a downstream counting collector. Then select top k via a bounded heap or sort only the entries. Example (simple and acceptable for k << distinct words):
```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public static List<Map.Entry<String,Long>> topK(Path file, int k) throws Exception {
  Map<String, Long> freq;
  try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(file)) {
    freq = lines
      .filter(s -> !s.startsWith("#"))
      .flatMap(s -> Arrays.stream(s.toLowerCase().split("\\W+")))
      .filter(w -> !w.isEmpty())
      .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), Collectors.counting()));
  }
  return freq.entrySet().stream()
      .sorted(Comparator
        .comparing(Map.Entry<String,Long>::getValue).reversed()
        .thenComparing(Map.Entry::getKey))
      .limit(k)
      .collect(Collectors.toList());
}
```
Justification: Files.lines streams without loading the entire file; groupingBy + counting builds only the frequency map; final sort occurs over distinct words only; limit(k) ensures we materialize at most k results after sorting.

