```
```css
.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 16px; }
.card { min-width: 280px; }
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
}
```
On 360px-wide screens, 2 columns are still attempted and overflow due to `.card { min-width: 280px; }`. Product wants: 1 col on small phones, 2 on tablets, 4 on desktop, with cards respecting their min width without overflow and filling rows neatly.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use CSS Grid's auto-fit/auto-fill with minmax to make columns responsive to available space instead of fixed repeats, and remove the conflicting min-width from the card.

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
}
.card { /* remove rigid min-width; let minmax handle it */
  /* optional intrinsic sizing */
}

@media (min-width: 1200px) { /* optional hard cap at 4 cols on very wide screens */
  .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
}
```
- Explanation: `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr))` ensures as many columns as fit given a 240px minimum, collapsing to 1 column on narrow phones and expanding to 2 and 4 as space allows, without overflow. Removing `.card { min-width: 280px; }` avoids fighting the grid's track sizing. The optional media query caps desktop at exactly 4 columns if mandated by design.

