The stacktrace mentions `LazyInitializationException` during JSON serialization of `items`. You must fix it with minimal performance cost and without reopening OSIV.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
One robust fix is to fetch the needed graph in the service and return a DTO. For example:

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
  @Query("select o from Order o left join fetch o.items where o.id = :id")
  Optional<Order> findWithItems(@Param("id") Long id);
}

@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public OrderDto find(Long id){
  Order o = repo.findWithItems(id).orElseThrow();
  return OrderDto.from(o); // map fields and items explicitly
}
```
Justification: `join fetch` guarantees `items` are initialized inside the transaction, preventing `LazyInitializationException`. Mapping to a DTO avoids exposing entities and prevents accidental lazy access to other associations during serialization. Alternatives: using `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "items")` is equivalent in effect and more declarative; acceptable. Annotating the controller with `@Transactional` is brittle because serialization happens after the controller returns, once the transaction may be closed; it also risks opening more than required. Re-enabling OSIV defers all lazy loads to the web layer and can hide N+1 issues; not recommended for performance. Eagerly changing the mapping to `EAGER` couples fetch strategy globally and may load too much in other use cases.

scenario_questions.scenario:
Your API currently accepts both POST and PUT for `/products`. The team is confused about idempotency and status codes. Current handlers:

```java
@PostMapping("/products")
public ResponseEntity<Product> create(@RequestBody Product p) {
  Product saved = repo.save(p); // id generated by DB
  return ResponseEntity.ok(saved);
}

@PutMapping("/products/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<Product> put(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody Product p) {
  p.setId(id);
  return ResponseEntity.ok(repo.save(p));
}
```
Clients sometimes send PUT with a new id that doesn't exist yet. Define the correct behavior and necessary changes so the API is RESTful and predictable.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
POST creates a new server-assigned idempotency-unsafe resource: return 201 Created with a `Location: /products/{id}` header and the representation. PUT is idempotent and targets a specific URI: if the product with `{id}` exists, update and return 200 OK (or 204 No Content). If it does not exist, create it with that id and return 201 Created with `Location`.

Changes:

```java
@PostMapping("/products")
public ResponseEntity<Product> create(@RequestBody @Valid Product p) {
  Product saved = repo.save(p);
  URI loc = URI.create("/products/" + saved.getId());
  return ResponseEntity.created(loc).body(saved);
}

@PutMapping("/products/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<Product> put(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody @Valid Product p) {
  if (p.getId()!=null && !p.getId().equals(id)) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).build();
  }
  boolean exists = repo.existsById(id);
  p.setId(id);
  Product saved = repo.save(p);
  if (!exists) {
    return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/products/"+id)).body(saved);
  }
  return ResponseEntity.ok(saved);
}
```
Justification: PUT is idempotent (repeating the same PUT yields the same state). Returning 201 on create-with-PUT is acceptable per RFC 9110. Enforcing path/body id agreement prevents accidental misrouting. POST remains non-idempotent and server assigns id.

